        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Sub-Saharan Africa</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Khoekhoe</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> south to the Cape; today the Nama of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, Botswana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> (Great and Little Namaqualand), the Damara of Namibia, the Orana clans and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> of South Africa, and the Gona clans within the Xhosa-speaking polities; the Richtersveld, a World Heritage Site.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">proto-Khoe c. 400 BCE–100 CE in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>; herding communities c. 200 BCE–300 CE, spreading south by 500 CE and south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> after 1000 CE; Portuguese contact in the 15th and 16th centuries; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonisation from the late 17th century; the Kat River Settlement 1829–1856; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> in German South-West Africa 1904–1907; apartheid to 1994.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">herds of sheep, goats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>; the Battle of Salt River (1510); Khoe languages related to those of Kalahari San; the Moon in religion, and Tsui’goab and ǁGaunab; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transhumance">transhumance</a> in the Richtersveld; the marksmen of the Kat River Settlement; the Nama and Damara clans and their captains</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Portuguese records of the 15th and 16th centuries; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Cape colonial records; travellers such as P. Kolben, A. Sparman, John Barrow and Emil Holub; Wilhelm Bleek’s Hottentot Fables and Tales (1864); Richard Elphick’s Khoikhoi and the Founding of White <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> (1977); archaeology (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadr">Sadr</a>); clan histories; genetic studies.</span></div>
