    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Khoekhoe</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orange-river.jpg" alt="Orange River" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Orange River <span class="kid-tale-cr">paffy, CC BY-SA 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>By one account, about 2,000 years ago in what is now southern Zimbabwe, San peoples met herders from East Africa.</p><p>From these meetings came the Khoekhoe, who spread south with their sheep and later their cattle, across the Orange River.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cattle.jpg" alt="Cattle" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Cattle <span class="kid-tale-cr">dr. Plamen Ivanov, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>For centuries they moved from place to place with their herds of sheep, goats and cattle.</p><p>Groups in the west called themselves ǀAwakhoen and those in the south ǀKx’abakhoena, both meaning “Red People”.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>In 1510, Khoekhoe riding on oxen beat a Portuguese party at Salt River and killed its leader.</p><p>Later, ships stopping in Table Bay traded tobacco, copper and iron with them for fresh meat.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eastern-cape.jpg" alt="Eastern Cape" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Eastern Cape <span class="kid-tale-cr">Felix Gottwald, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Dutch settlers took their grazing land for farms, and smallpox, a disease brought by Europeans, killed many.</p><p>In 1829 some Khoekhoe were given land of their own at Kat River, on the Cape’s eastern frontier.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/group-areas-act.jpg" alt="Group Areas Act" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Group Areas Act <span class="kid-tale-cr">Photo Division, Government of India, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>In a war with German forces from 1904 to 1907, more than half of the Nama people may have died.</p><p>Later, under apartheid, unfair laws in South Africa kept people apart by race and forced many families from their homes.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transhumance.jpg" alt="Transhumance" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Transhumance <span class="kid-tale-cr">CamDib, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Today the Nama, Damara, Griqua and Orana nations live on, and many work to bring back their culture and land.</p><p>In the Richtersveld, a World Heritage Site, herders still move their animals from pasture to pasture, as in the past.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>By one account a proto-Khoe people emerged between 400 BCE and 100 CE in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-people">San peoples</a> met herding communities drifting south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>; with their sheep and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, Khoekhoe herders spread south to the Cape.</li><li>In 1510 ox-mounted ǃUriǁ’aekua defeated the party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a> at the Battle of Salt River, killing him and fifty of his men.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">Smallpox</a>, land seizure and war under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule broke up Khoekhoe society, and in German South-West Africa from 1904 to 1907 over 10,000 Nama, more than half the people, may have died.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The International Astronomical Union has named the star Mu¹ Scorpii Xami di mûra, “eyes of the lion”, after its traditional name in the Khoekhoe language.</p></aside>
    </section>

